it's taken more than a week for these  images to reach the outside world  covered in dust clearly in shock but at  first confirmation some civilians  survived the bombing of a theater in  mariopol  eyewitnesses say at least 300 people  lost their lives it's not clear how many  more may still be buried here  the building had been packed with more  than a thousand civilians the word  children clearly visible from the air  diana lived next to the theater but  escaped before the bombing  everywhere it was flooded with people i  know that there were pregnant women that  were brought from that maternity ward in  the third house hospital that was bombed  the days before they were brought to the  theater because it's like a big building  they put these women uh with kids or  pregnant women in these dressing rooms  for for the actors  and it was in this this part which was  born and they were in the theater when  it was yeah they were in the 30th they  were right in on the  exactly like the point where it was hit  diana says there are now so many bodies  they're not buried anymore and body  parts hang from trees  those who are still living this  nightmare have little way of being heard  british photographer max clark recently  escaped with these images and stories of  suffering the bodies are largely being  buried by now but there's still some you  can certainly smell them in piles of  rubble that are inaccessible  there are dogs feeding on chunks of bone  human bone  bits of people  there are freshly dug graves everywhere  their cries for help cannot be heard but  the people of mariopol need saving from  this living hell  sally lockwood sky news la viv in  western ukraine
 

